!!Abdera!! (Greek: Άβδηρα) has 17,610 inhabitants (2021), and it has been abandoned since some time after the 4th century AD. Both statements are true, because the name belongs to two places. One is a [[ municipality | municipalities-and-communities-of-greece ]] in the [[ Xanthi regional unit ]] of [[ Thrace | western-thrace ]], [[ Greece ]]. The other is the ancient //[[ polis ]]// the municipality was named in honor of: in [[ classical antiquity ]] a major [[ Greek | ancient-greece ]] city on the [[ Thracian | thrace ]] coast, whose ruin the municipality now contains.

The polis lay 17 km east-northeast of the mouth of the [[ Nestos River ]], almost directly opposite the island of [[ Thasos ]]. It began as a colony planted in Thracian territory that was then unsettled and not yet part of Hellas, during the age of [[ Greek colonization ]]. The city that grew from it became of major importance in ancient Greece. After the 4th century AD it declined, contracted to its [[ acropolis ]], and was abandoned, never to be reoccupied except by archaeologists.

In the [[ Early Middle Ages ]] a new settlement grew up near the ancient city. It was called Polystylon (Greek: Πολύστυλον),[[ ^3 ]] and was later considered the New Abdera (Greek: Νέα Άβδηρα). The present municipality is younger still: it was [[ synoecized | synoecism ]] in 2011 from three earlier municipalities that between them take in a number of modern settlements. Its seat is not Abdera at all but the town of [[ Genisea ]].[[ ^4 ]]

== Name

The name //Abdera// is of [[ Phoenician | phoenician-language ]] origin, and the Thracian city was not the only place to carry it: in antiquity it was shared by [[ Abdera, Spain ]] and by a town near [[ Carthage ]] in [[ North Africa ]].[[ ^5 ]][[ ^a ]] It was variously [[ Hellenized | hellenization ]] as Ἄβδηρα (//Ábdēra//), Αὔδηρα (//Aúdēra//),[[ ^7 ]] Ἄβδαρα (//Ábdara//),[[ ^8 ]] Ἄβδηρον (//Ábdēron//),[[ ^7 ]] and Ἄβδηρος (//Ábdēros//),[[ ^9 ]] before it was [[ Latinized | latinisation-of-names ]] as //Abdera//.[[ ^7 ]] [[ Greek legend | greek-mythology ]] had its own explanation: an eponymous [[ Abderus ]] fell nearby, and [[ Hercules ]] founded a city on the spot to memorialize him.[[ ^10 ]]

The present-day town is written !!Avdira!! (Άβδηρα) and pronounced [[ [ˈavðira\] | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help%3AIPA%2FGreek ]] in [[ modern Greek ]].

== History



== Antiquity

The Greeks founded Abdera twice, and the first attempt failed. Before either, the [[ Phoenicians | phoenicia ]] apparently began settling the site at some point before the mid-7th century,[[ ^5 ]] and the town kept Phoenician standards in its coinage long afterwards. Its neighbours were Thracian: the [[ Sapaeans ]] ([[ Ancient Greek ]], "Σαπαίοι") were a [[ Thracian | thracians ]][[ ^11 ]] tribe living close to the Greek city.

The Greek settlement began as a colony from [[ Klazomenai ]], traditionally dated to 654 BC, and it did not last. (Evidence in 7th-century-BC [[ Greek pottery | pottery-of-ancient-greece ]] tends to support the traditional date, but the exact timing remains uncertain.)[[ ^12 ]] [[ Herodotus ]] reports that the colony's leader was Timesios, and that within his generation the [[ Thracians ]] had expelled the colonists; the later Abderans from Teos nevertheless honored Timesios as a [[ local protective spirit | hero-ancient-greece ]].[[ ^13 ]] Other writers tell it differently. [[ Plutarch ]] and [[ Aelian | claudius-aelianus ]] relate that Timesios grew insufferable to his colonists because he wanted to do everything himself; when one of their children let him know how they all really felt, he quit the settlement in disgust. Modern scholars have tried to split the difference between the two accounts of early Abdera's failure by making the latter the reason Timesios left Klazomenai in the first place.[[ ^14 ]]

[[ Strabo ]] still describes Abdera as "a Thracian city"[[ ^15 ]] at the time of [[ Anacreon ]] and the migration of people from [[ Teos ]] to the area. That migration was the second, successful founding. In 544 BC the majority of the people of [[ Teos ]], the poet [[ Anacreon ]] among them, moved to Abdera to escape the [[ Persian | persian-empire ]] invasion of their homeland.[[ ^16 ]][[ ^17 ]] The link to Teos is stamped on the coins: the chief type, a //[[ griffon | griffin ]]//, is identical with that of Teos, and the rich silver coinage is noted for the beauty and variety of its reverse types.[[ ^18 ]]

The Persians the Teians had fled reached Abdera too. In 513 and 512 BC, under [[ Darius | darius-the-great ]], they conquered the city; by then it seems to have become a place of considerable importance, and it is named among the cities that had the expensive honour of entertaining the great king on his march into Greece.[[ ^19 ]] In 492 BC, after the [[ Ionian Revolt ]], the Persians conquered Abdera again, still under [[ Darius I ]] but led by his general [[ Mardonius | mardonius-general ]]. Xerxes, on his flight after the [[ Battle of Salamis ]], stopped at Abdera and acknowledged the hospitality of its inhabitants by presenting them with a tiara and scimitar of gold.[[ ^20 ]] [[ Thucydides ]][[ ^21 ]] gives Abdera as the westernmost limit of the [[ Odrysian kingdom ]] when at its height, at the beginning of the [[ Peloponnesian War ]]. The city later became part of the [[ Delian League ]] and fought on the side of Athens in that war.[[ ^22 ]]

Abdera was a wealthy city, the third richest in the League, because it was a prime port for trade with the interior of Thrace and the [[ Odrysian kingdom ]].[[ ^12 ]] In 408 BC it was reduced under the power of Athens by [[ Thrasybulus ]], then one of the Athenian generals in that quarter.[[ ^23 ]]

Wealth made it a prize, and it was sacked repeatedly: by the [[ Triballi ]] in 376 BC, by [[ Philip II of Macedon ]] in 350 BC, later by [[ Lysimachos of Thrace | lysimachus ]],[[ ^17 ]] by the [[ Seleucids | seleucid-empire ]], by the [[ Ptolemies | ptolemaic-kingdom ]], and again by the Macedonians. In 170 BC the Roman armies and those of [[ Eumenes II of Pergamon | eumenes-ii ]] besieged and sacked it.

The town seems to have declined in importance after the middle of the 4th century BC.[[ ^18 ]] Its later fame was of an unwelcome kind. Cicero, writing to [[ Atticus | atticus-philosopher ]] about a debate in the Senate, used the city as a byword for stupidity: "Here was Abdera, but I wasn't silent" ("Hic, Abdera non tacente me").[[ ^24 ]] The //[[ Philogelos ]]//, a Greek-language joke book compiled in the 4th century AD, gives a whole chapter to jokes about Abderans, who are stereotyped as stupid, superstitious, and literal-minded.[[ ^25 ]] The joke sits oddly beside the roll of citizens: the philosophers [[ Democritus ]], [[ Protagoras ]][[ ^17 ]] and [[ Anaxarchus ]], the historian and philosopher [[ Hecataeus of Abdera ]], and the lyric poet [[ Anacreon ]]. [[ Pliny the Elder ]] speaks of Abdera as being, in his time, a free city.[[ ^26 ]]

Abdera had flourished in ancient times mainly for two reasons: the large area of its territory and its highly strategic position. The city controlled two great road passages, one along the Nestos river and the other through the mountains north of Xanthi, and through its ports ran the sea road that led from Troas to the Thracian coast and then the Macedonian.[[ ^27 ]]

The ruins of the town may still be seen on Cape Balastra (40°56'1.02"N 24°58'21.81"E). They cover seven small hills and extend from an eastern to a western harbor; on the southwestern hills are the remains of the medieval settlement of Polystylon (Greek: Πολύστυλον). From the 9th century, [[ Byzantine | byzantine-empire ]] Polystylon was an [[ episcopal see ]] under the jurisdiction of the metropolitan bishop of [[ Philippi ]]. By the end of the 14th century it had fallen under [[ Ottoman | ottoman-empire ]] rule.[[ ^3 ]]

== Modern

The modern Avdira is a creation of the twentieth century. It was established as an administrative unit (community) in 1924, consisting of the villages Avdira, [[ Myrodato ]] (Kalfalar), [[ Pezoula ]], [[ Giona | giona-xanthi ]], Veloni and [[ Mandra | mandra-xanthi ]]; Myrodato and Mandra became separate communities in 1928.[[ ^28 ]] The municipality Avdira was formed in 1997 by the merger of the former communities Avdira, Mandra, Myrodato and [[ Nea Kessani ]].[[ ^4 ]] At the 2011 local government reform it merged with the former municipalities [[ Selero ]] and [[ Vistonida ]], and the town [[ Genisea ]] became its seat.[[ ^4 ]][[ ^29 ]]

The municipality has an area of 352.047 km^^2^^, the municipal unit 161.958 km^^2^^.[[ ^30 ]] The municipal unit Avdira is subdivided into the communities Avdira, Mandra, Myrodato and Nea Kessani, and the community Avdira consists of the settlements Avdira, Giona, Lefkippos, Pezoula and Skala.

== Landmarks

Landmarks of Abdera include the [[ Archaeological Museum of Abdera ]], the [[ Kütüklü Baba Tekke ]], and Agios Ioannis Beach (also //Paralia Avdiron//) near the village Lefkippos.

